# The ledger of `sftp`: what a client of SFTP version 3 lets a program do,
# one block per capability, and what Rebel answers.
#
# Go 1.27, Rust 1.100 and Python 3.15 carry no SFTP in their standard
# libraries, so every `rust` and `python` line is `-`. The yardstick is Go's
# `github.com/pkg/sftp` v1.13.12 (`go` below), every entry listed by `go doc
# -all` run 2026-11-24 in a module cache inside the lane's scratch. The
# protocol is draft-ietf-secsh-filexfer-02 and OpenSSH's `PROTOCOL` §§ 4.1
# to 4.12 (read 2026-11-24), and the second implementation the tests are
# held to is OpenSSH 10.5p1, this Mac's own: its `sshd` run as the user on
# 127.0.0.1 with a configuration of its own and `/usr/libexec/sftp-server`
# as the subsystem, whose `-Q requests` listed every request this package
# sends; every file the tests write through SFTP is read back through the
# system's own calls, and every one the system writes read through SFTP.

capability sftp/session  a session on a channel of an `ssh` client: the subsystem started, version 3 agreed, the server's extensions read
  heroes  sftp.start, sftp.close, sftp.Session, sftp.extensions, sftp.has_extension
  go      NewClient, NewClientPipe, Client, Client.Close, Client.Wait, Client.HasExtension, ClientOption
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    one client carries any number of sessions and other channels beside them, each call taking the client and the session in their places; an extension is used only where the server announced it with the version `PROTOCOL` § 4.2 says a client must check, so a later incompatible version is `unsupported`, never misread; a frame the session cannot read ends it, every later call `closed` with the reason

capability sftp/pipeline  many requests in flight, sized to the server's limits
  heroes  sftp.read, sftp.write, sftp.read_bytes, sftp.write_bytes
  go      MaxPacket, MaxPacketChecked, MaxPacketUnchecked, MaxConcurrentRequestsPerFile, UseConcurrentReads, UseConcurrentWrites
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    requests of 32 KiB, 64 in flight, the defaults of OpenSSH's own `sftp` (`-B`, `-R`, its manual read 2026-11-24), lowered where `limits@openssh.com` (`PROTOCOL` § 4.8) announces less, since a server may end the connection past them; 64 answers of 32 KiB fill the `ssh` channel's window of 2 MiB and no more; a read whose end is not known opens at one request and doubles with each full answer; answers are matched by number in whatever order they come, and an answer to a request never sent ends the session; where Go lets a program choose the sizes, Rebel takes OpenSSH's and the server's

capability sftp/open  a file opened to read, write, append, make new or both, and closed
  heroes  sftp.open, sftp.create, sftp.open_with, sftp.close_file, sftp.File
  go      Client.Open, Client.Create, Client.OpenFile, File, File.Close, File.Name, FileOpenFlags
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the accesses are `fs.Access`'s, so a program opens a remote file as it opens a local one; a file made takes the permission asked less the server's mask; a name taken for `.create_new` is `exists`, told by a look at the path, since version 3's status names it a bare failure

capability sftp/read-write  bytes read and written at an offset, and whole files
  heroes  sftp.read, sftp.write, sftp.read_bytes, sftp.write_bytes
  go      File.ReadAt, File.WriteAt, File.ReadFrom, File.ReadFromWithConcurrency, File.WriteTo
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a read ends short only at the end of the file: an answer shorter than asked is asked again from where it stopped, and the pieces must cover the bytes with no gap or the answer is `invalid`; a write past the end leaves zeros, as the system's `pwrite` does; checked with 3 MB and 5 MB files written through SFTP and read by the system, and back

capability sftp/attributes  what a path or an open file names: kind, size, permission, owner, group, times, a link followed or not
  heroes  sftp.info, sftp.link_info, sftp.file_info, sftp.Attributes
  go      Client.Stat, Client.Lstat, File.Stat, FileStat, FileStat.AccessTime, FileStat.FileMode, FileStat.ModTime, FileAttrFlags, FileInfoUidGid, FileInfoExtendedData, StatExtended, S_IFMT, UseFstat
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    each field is `not_found` where the server left it out, which version 3 allows, never a zero that reads as a value; the kind is `fs.Kind`, read from the type bits POSIX numbers; times are whole seconds, all version 3 carries; checked field by field against the system's own `stat` of the same files

capability sftp/set-attributes  permission, owner, times and size set by path or on an open file
  heroes  sftp.set_permission, sftp.set_owner, sftp.set_times, sftp.truncate, sftp.set_file_permission, sftp.set_file_owner, sftp.set_file_times, sftp.truncate_file
  go      Client.Chmod, Client.Chown, Client.Chtimes, Client.Truncate, File.Chmod, File.Chown, File.Truncate
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    each call sets its own attributes alone; an owner of -1 is kept, written as 2^32 - 1 as POSIX's `chown` reads it; a time outside 1970 to 2106 is `invalid` before anything is sent, version 3 carrying a `uint32` of seconds; a folder truncated is `is_a_directory`

capability sftp/set-link-attributes  a link's own permission, owner and times set, never what it names
  heroes  sftp.set_link_permission, sftp.set_link_owner, sftp.set_link_times
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `lsetstat@openssh.com` (`PROTOCOL` § 4.7), `unsupported` where the server does not announce it; macOS keeps a link's own permission, checked by the system's `lstat`

capability sftp/extended-attributes-set  a file's named attribute extensions written
  heroes  pending
  go      Client.SetExtendedData, File.SetExtendedData
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the pairs are read (`Attributes.extended`); a write of them waits for a server that keeps them, since OpenSSH's, the one the tests reach, announces none and sends none

capability sftp/folders  a folder listed, made, made with those above it, removed
  heroes  sftp.read_dir, sftp.Listed, sftp.make_dir, sftp.make_dir_with, sftp.make_dir_all, sftp.remove_dir
  go      Client.ReadDir, Client.ReadDirContext, Client.Mkdir, Client.MkdirAll, Client.RemoveDirectory
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a listing is read to its end and sorted by its names' bytes, `.` and `..` left out, as `fs.read_dir` answers, a name that is not UTF-8 `not_text`; 250 names across OpenSSH's answers checked against the system's own listing; a folder taken is `exists`, one holding something `not_empty` and a file `not_a_directory`, each told by a look at the path where version 3's status names a bare failure or no such file

capability sftp/remove  a file or a link removed, and a tree with what it holds
  heroes  sftp.remove, sftp.remove_all
  go      Client.Remove, Client.RemoveAll
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `remove` takes a file or a link and answers a folder `is_a_directory`, keeping the protocol's two requests apart, where Go's `Remove` tries both; `remove_all` removes a link inside, never what it names, and answers `file_not_found` for a path that names nothing, as `fs.remove_all` does

capability sftp/rename  a path renamed: never over a name taken, or over it at once
  heroes  sftp.rename, sftp.posix_rename
  go      Client.Rename, Client.PosixRename
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `rename` is version 3's, which never replaces, a name taken `exists`; `posix_rename` is `posix-rename@openssh.com` (`PROTOCOL` § 4.3), POSIX's `rename`, as Go's two are

capability sftp/links  symbolic links made and read, hard links made
  heroes  sftp.symlink, sftp.read_link, sftp.hard_link
  go      Client.Symlink, Client.ReadLink, Client.Link
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    OpenSSH's server reads `SYMLINK`'s two paths in the order other than the draft's (`PROTOCOL` § 4.1), so the target goes first, checked by the system's `readlink` of the link made; `hard_link` is `hardlink@openssh.com` (§ 4.5); a file that is no link read as one is `invalid`

capability sftp/paths  a path made absolute and clean, `~` expanded, a user's home
  heroes  sftp.resolve, sftp.expand, sftp.home
  go      Client.RealPath, Client.Getwd
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `resolve(".")` is the folder the session starts in, which is Go's `Getwd`; `expand` is `expand-path@openssh.com` (§ 4.9) and `home` `home-directory` (§ 4.11); OpenSSH answers an unknown user's home a bare failure, `failed`, and its `~` `no such file`, `file_not_found`

capability sftp/sync  an open file's bytes written to the server's disk
  heroes  sftp.sync
  go      File.Sync
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `fsync@openssh.com` (§ 4.6), `unsupported` where the server does not announce it

capability sftp/volume  the space and the files of the volume a path or an open file stands on
  heroes  sftp.volume, sftp.file_volume, sftp.Volume
  go      Client.StatVFS, StatVFS, StatVFS.FreeSpace, StatVFS.TotalSpace, StatVFS.MarshalBinary
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `statvfs@openssh.com` and `fstatvfs@openssh.com` (§ 4.4); the block counts are turned into bytes once, a product past 2^63 - 1 `invalid`, so a program multiplies nothing; the size checked against the system's own `statvfs` through `fs.space`

capability sftp/copy  bytes copied between two open files by the server itself
  heroes  sftp.copy_range
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `copy-data` (§ 4.10); a count of 0 asks nothing, where the extension reads 0 as to the end of the file, so no number means all; one handle both sides is refused before anything is sent

capability sftp/names  the names of users and groups by their numbers
  heroes  sftp.user_names, sftp.group_names
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `users-groups-by-id@openssh.com` (§ 4.12); a number the server knows no name for is left out of the map, where the extension answers it empty

capability sftp/failure  each status of version 3 answered by a code of Rebel's closed vocabulary
  heroes  sftp.FILE_NOT_FOUND, sftp.EXISTS, sftp.NOT_A_DIRECTORY, sftp.IS_A_DIRECTORY, sftp.NOT_EMPTY, sftp.PERMISSION, sftp.INVALID, sftp.NOT_TEXT, sftp.CLOSED, sftp.TIMEOUT, sftp.UNSUPPORTED, sftp.FAILED
  go      StatusError, StatusError.Error, StatusError.FxCode, TransferError, ErrSSHFxOk, ErrSSHFxEOF, ErrSSHFxNoSuchFile, ErrSSHFxPermissionDenied, ErrSSHFxFailure, ErrSSHFxBadMessage, ErrSSHFxNoConnection, ErrSSHFxConnectionLost, ErrSSHFxOpUnsupported, ErrSshFxOk, ErrSshFxEof, ErrSshFxNoSuchFile, ErrSshFxPermissionDenied, ErrSshFxFailure, ErrSshFxBadMessage, ErrSshFxNoConnection, ErrSshFxConnectionLost, ErrSshFxOpUnsupported, EBADF, ErrInternalInconsistency, InternalInconsistency
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `NO_SUCH_FILE` is `file_not_found`, `PERMISSION_DENIED` `permission`, `FAILURE` `failed`, `BAD_MESSAGE` `invalid`, `NO_CONNECTION` and `CONNECTION_LOST` `closed`, `OP_UNSUPPORTED` `unsupported`; the codes version 3 cannot name, `exists`, `not_empty`, `not_a_directory` and `is_a_directory`, are told by one look at the path after the failure, as each status of OpenSSH 10.5p1 was measured; the server's own words stay in every message

capability sftp/walk-glob  a folder's tree walked and the server's paths matched by a pattern
  heroes  pending
  go      Client.Walk, Client.Glob, Match, ErrBadPattern
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `read_dir` and `link_info` give a program each step; a walk and a glob are written once `fs`'s walks and glob read folders through a reader the program names, so the local and the remote share one walker

capability sftp/server  the server's side: requests served from the program's own files or handlers
  heroes  pending
  go      Server, NewServer, Server.Close, Server.Serve, ServerOption, ReadOnly, WindowsRootEnumeratesDrives, WithAllocator, WithDebug, WithMaxTxPacket, WithServerWorkingDirectory, RequestServer, NewRequestServer, RequestServer.Close, RequestServer.Serve, RequestServerOption, WithRSAllocator, WithRSMaxTxPacket, WithStartDirectory, Handlers, InMemHandler, Request, NewRequest, Request.AttrFlags, Request.Attributes, Request.Context, Request.Pflags, Request.WithContext, FileCmder, FileLister, FileReader, FileWriter, ListerAt, LstatFileLister, NameLookupFileLister, OpenFileWriter, PosixRenameFileCmder, ReadlinkFileLister, RealPathFileLister, StatVFSFileCmder, WriterAtReaderAt, SftpServerWorkerCount, MaxFilelist, SetSFTPExtensions
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    it stands on `ssh`'s server side, itself a `pending` block of `ssh.ledger`

capability sftp/versions-4-6  versions 4 to 6 of the protocol
  heroes  refused: OpenSSH, the server this client is written for, speaks version 3 alone, and a server of a later version answers 3 to a client asking 3 (each draft's § on version negotiation); a server of version 3 that refused a client of 3 would make it wrong
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped cursor  a file's own place read and moved (Go's `File.Read`, `File.Write`, `File.Seek`): every read and write names its offset, so two parts of a program never share a hidden place
dropped path-text  a remote path's parts joined and split (Go's `Join`, `Split`, `Client.Join`): `path` with POSIX's rules, which every SFTP server's paths follow
dropped stderr  the subsystem's errors copied out (Go's `CopyStderrTo`): a server's words for a person at the server, where every answer the program needs is a status with its message
